    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d665">

                <pb facs="0606" n="597" xml:id="pg597"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/5960: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Algiers</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Cole</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Algiers</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-10-10T15:00:00+01:00">October 10, 1940—3
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 7 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>124. General Weygand<anchor n="70" xml:id="d665-f1"/> arrived yesterday afternoon
                    to establish headquarters here with a small staff and the title “Delegate
                    General of the (Vichy) Government in French Africa” for the briefly announced
                    purpose of assuring the protection of the French African bloc and furthering its
                    economic prosperity by coordinating the administrative and political action of
                    the Residents and Governors General.</p>
                <p>The habitually un-city [<hi rendition="#italic">sic</hi>] population received him
                    with unusual apathy and the atmosphere at the official reception was
                    strained.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cole</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d665-f1" n="70" xml:id="d665-f1-content">
                <p>Gen. Maxime Weygand, formerly Minister of National Defense in Marshal Pétain’s
                    Cabinet.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
